  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA shortwave will be diving from the Quebec and Ontario southeast\ninto the Northeast for today. This shortwave will help to\ncontinue to carve out a trough over the Northeast, allowing for\ncooler temps aloft to pour into the region.  \n\nAlthough moisture is limited, this digging upper level shortwave\nwill allow for some isolated to widely scattered showers and\nthunderstorms to develop during peak heating today. Instability\nlooks very limited as well, with both the 00z HRRR and 00z SPC \nHREF suggesting around 500 J/kg of SBCAPE across the area. \nStill, this will be enough for a rumble or two, but strong \nstorms aren't anticipated today due to the limited instability \nand poor lapse rates aloft, which will limit how tall storms can\ngrow. Coverage looks sparse, so will keep POPs around 15%-30%\nacross the area, with the highest POPs in northern and eastern\nareas. Any showers or storms will diminish towards evening with\nthe loss of daytime heating.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWith the cooler temps in place aloft, there will be a noticeable\nchange in high and low temps across the region for this weekend.\nDaytime temps will generally be in the 70s with 50s at night\n(some 40s across the high terrain). This will be some the\ncoolest temperatures we have seen so far this month across the\nregion as highs have been in the 80s each day so far this month. \nDewpoints will be much lower on Saturday, with values down into\nthe 50s, especially during afternoon mixing, so it will\ncertainly feel more comfortable than recent days well.\n\nAlthough it will be dry and cool for Saturday, moisture looks to\nreturn for Sunday as the next system approaches from the west.\nBased on the latest model guidance, an upper level disturbance\nand associated surface frontal system will move eastward across\nthe region from the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley within the \nzonal flow aloft. NBM POPs are in the likely to categorical\nrange for Sunday into Sunday night with periods of showers and\nembedded thunderstorms. Some rain may linger into early Monday,\nbut clearing is expected during the day on Monday. \n\nLatest NBM shows about a 25% to 50% chance for exceeding 1 inch\nacross the region for most of the area, but probabilities are \ngenerally under 20% to see over 2 inches. With some embedded \nheavier bursts possible and soils still on the saturated side, \nthere remains a risk for some flash flooding, especially within \nurban and poor drainage areas. WPC continues a Marginal Risk \n(Level 1 of 4) for Excessive Rainfall across the region.",
